==Pre-match==
===Venue===
Due to the ongoing reconstruction of Wembley Stadium, the match was played at the [[Millennium Stadium]] in [[Cardiff]] for the fourth year in a row.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cardiff set for Cup final |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3735107.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=21 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> The stadium was built in 1998 ahead of the [[1999 Rugby World Cup]] in Wales, with a capacity of 72,500. While it was being built, the [[Wales national rugby union team]] played its home matches at the [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|old Wembley Stadium]], so after Wembley was torn down in 2000, the Millennium Stadium was selected to host the finals of the FA Cup, the [[Football League Cup|League Cup]] and the [[Football League play-offs]] until at least 2003.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup heads for Cardiff |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/wales/1099821.stm |publisher=BBC News (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=4 January 2001 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> Delays to the construction of the [[Wembley Stadium|new Wembley]] meant that deal was later extended until 2006.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA books Cardiff as final back-up |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/4150926.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=14 August 2005 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref>

===Referee===
The referee for the final was [[Jeff Winter]] from [[Middlesbrough]], [[North Yorkshire]], who was officiating in his last match as a professional referee, having reached the mandatory retirement age of 45.<ref>{{cite news |title=Final nod for Winter |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3603305.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=6 April 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Stuart |last=Roach |title=Winter in wonderland |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3736765.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> Winter's [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]] were [[Roger East (referee)|Roger East]] and Tony Green, while [[Matt Messias]] was the [[Assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth official]].

===Broadcasting===
The match was broadcast live on television in the [[United Kingdom]] on both [[BBC One]] and [[Sky Sports|Sky Sports 1]].<ref>{{cite news |title=BBC happy with Final figures |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1972387.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=7 May 2002 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> The BBC broadcast was presented by [[Gary Lineker]], with [[Alan Hansen]], [[Peter Schmeichel]] and [[Michael Owen]] in the studio, and commentary from [[John Motson]] in his 25th FA Cup Final as lead commentator.<ref name="bbc_broadcast">{{cite news |title=BBC has the Cup final covered! |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3725153.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=20 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> The BBC also provided live radio coverage on [[BBC Radio 5 Live]], presented by [[Mark Pougatch]], with commentary from [[Alan Green (broadcaster)|Alan Green]] and [[Mike Ingham]], and analysis from [[Jimmy Armfield]] and [[Steve Claridge]].<ref name="bbc_broadcast"/>

==Match==
===Team selection===
Manchester United goalkeeper [[Roy Carroll]] appeared to have earned his place in the team for the FA Cup final after a run of good performances between the end of March and mid-April 2004, conceding just two goals in five games, including the semi-final win over [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] on 3 April.<ref>{{cite news |title=Carroll set for FA Cup slot |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/3613339.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=9 April 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> However, a 1–0 defeat away to [[Portsmouth F.C.|Portsmouth]] on 17 April led to a recall for American goalkeeper [[Tim Howard]] for the last five games of the season. Although Howard conceded three goals in that time, he retained his place in the team for the cup final. Following the recurrence of a knee injury against Arsenal on 28 March, striker [[Ruud van Nistelrooy]] was only able to play in one match in April 2004 and was expected to miss the last two league games against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] and [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] to ensure his fitness for the final;<ref>{{cite news |title=Ruud rested until Cup final |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/3688641.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=6 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> however, he recovered in time to play and score in both games, guaranteeing his place up front against Millwall. One surprise selection saw [[Darren Fletcher]] named in central midfield alongside [[Roy Keane]] ahead of both [[Nicky Butt]] and [[Phil Neville]].

Millwall went into the final with doubts over the fitness of player-manager [[Dennis Wise]] (knee), captain [[Kevin Muscat]] (knee) and midfielder [[Paul Ifill]] (groin);<ref>{{cite news |title=Wise receives Cup boost |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/m/millwall/3604749.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=6 April 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> Wise and Ifill recovered in time to play in the final, but Muscat was ruled out for the rest of the season.<ref>{{cite news |first1=David |last1=Plummer |first2=Daniel |last2=Taylor |title=Muscat suffers final injury |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/apr/09/newsstory.sport2 |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=9 April 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> [[Bob Peeters]], [[Andy Roberts (footballer)|Andy Roberts]], [[Tony Warner]] and [[Charley Hearn]] also missed the game through injury, while striker [[Danny Dichio]] was suspended.<ref>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Fletcher |title=Lions ready for dream date |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3725753.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=20 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Former Millwall striker Peeters bids to end Charlton's derby woes |url=http://www.newsshopper.co.uk/sport/11606680.Former_Millwall_striker_Peeters_bids_to_end_Charlton_s_derby_woes/ |website=newsshopper.co.uk |publisher=Newsquest |date=17 November 2014 |access-date=18 August 2017 }}</ref> Because they were unable to play in the game, and with manager Wise in the starting line-up, Muscat and Warner led the Millwall team out for the national anthem before kick-off.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup final clockwatch |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3738415.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref>

===Summary===
The first chances of the match fell to Manchester United's [[Paul Scholes]], who took a couple of long-range efforts at goal – one went just wide while another was tipped away by Millwall goalkeeper [[Andy Marshall]]. Scholes was presented with another opportunity on the six-yard line, when a [[rabona]] cross from [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] found him unmarked; the midfielder seemed as surprised by the cross as everyone else and completely missed the ball with his hooked shot.<ref name="bbc_report">{{cite news |title=Man Utd win FA Cup |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3725063.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=10 November 2015 }}</ref>

Millwall's only chance of the first half came when [[Paul Ifill]] broke down the right-hand side of the pitch and arrowed in towards the penalty area, only to have his shot blocked.<ref name="bbc_report"/> They looked to have survived the first half without conceding until a minute before the interval, when [[Roy Keane]] played in [[Gary Neville]] as the right-back moved into the penalty area and Neville chipped a cross back across the box; Millwall player-manager [[Dennis Wise]] waited for the ball to arrive, but in doing so, he allowed Ronaldo to steal in and head the ball past Marshall, to give United a 1–0 lead going into the break.<ref name="bbc_report"/>

Manchester United went 2–0 up 20 minutes into the second half, when [[Ryan Giggs]] went on a run down the right wing into the Millwall box only to be brought down by [[David Livermore]]. Referee [[Jeff Winter]] awarded a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] for the foul and [[Ruud van Nistelrooy]] scored with a powerful shot into the top-corner to the goalkeeper's right.<ref name="guardian_report">{{cite news |first=Kevin |last=McCarra |title=United triumph by taking the job seriously |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/may/24/match.manchesterunited |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=24 May 2004 |access-date=10 November 2015 }}</ref> United now held a comfortable advantage and Millwall struggled to find a way back into the match. United's defence kept [[Neil Harris (footballer, born 1977)|Neil Harris]] and [[Tim Cahill]] at bay, and the Red Devils eventually scored a third when Giggs went on a run down the left and crossed for Van Nistelrooy to tap in from three yards out. There were suggestions that Van Nistelrooy was offside at the moment of Giggs' pass, but television replays showed his feet were grounded in an onside position.<ref name="guardian_report"/>

Millwall had a chance near the end when substitute [[Mark McCammon]] almost found a way through United's defence, while Scholes had a late effort for the Reds, before [[Curtis Weston]] (aged 17 years and 119 days) replaced Wise to become the youngest player to appear in an FA Cup final,<ref name="bbc_report"/> beating the record set by [[James F. M. Prinsep]] of [[Clapham Rovers F.C.|Clapham Rovers]], when he appeared in the [[1879 FA Cup Final]] at the age of 17 years and 245 days. The match finished 3–0 to Manchester United, their 11th success in the FA Cup.<ref name="bbc_report"/>

