==Post-match==
Manchester United were presented with the FA Cup by [[England national football team|England]] manager [[Sven-Göran Eriksson]], who selected [[Ruud van Nistelrooy]] as the man of the match. For the trophy presentation, the Manchester United squad changed into shirts bearing the name and squad number of [[Jimmy Davis (footballer)|Jimmy Davis]], who died in a road accident while on loan to [[Watford F.C.|Watford]] in August 2003.<ref>{{cite news |title=Players remember Davis |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3739335.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> Eriksson's selection of Van Nistelrooy as man of the match was met with criticism from some members of the media, with both the BBC and ''[[The Guardian]]'' naming [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] as the game's best player.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup final player ratings |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3738753.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Matt |last=Scott |title=Time to Wise up and stand down |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/may/24/sport.comment2 |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=24 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> Ronaldo received praise from both his manager Alex Ferguson and teammate [[Gary Neville]] after the game,<ref>{{cite news |title=Neville hails Ronaldo |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3725109.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> as well as BBC pundit Alan Hansen<ref>{{cite news |first=Alan |last=Hansen |title=Wing wizard lights up final |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3739251.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> and ''The Guardian''{{'}}s [[Andy Gray (footballer, born 1955)|Andy Gray]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Andy |last=Gray |title=How United's wide boys turned the flanks and the game |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/may/24/sport.comment4 |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=24 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref>

Dennis Wise claimed Ronaldo's goal at the end of the first half proved the turning point in the match, but he felt there were plenty of positives for his team to take from the game.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wise positive in defeat |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3733231.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=22 May 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> It was [[Tim Cahill]]'s last game for Millwall, as he signed for [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] in July 2004,<ref>{{cite news |title=Cahill signs for Everton |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/e/everton/3899609.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=23 July 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> and [[Nicky Butt]]'s last game for Manchester United, as he signed for [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] later that same month.<ref>{{cite news |title=Newcastle sign Butt |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/n/newcastle_united/3928073.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=29 July 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref>

Since Manchester United had already qualified for the [[2004–05 UEFA Champions League]] by virtue of their league position, Millwall qualified for the first round of the [[2004–05 UEFA Cup]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Lions confirmed in Uefa Cup |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/m/millwall/3620367.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=12 April 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref> Club owner [[Theo Paphitis]] said the club had made around £2.5&nbsp;million for making it to the FA Cup final, but that the club could stand to lose money if they failed to reach the group stage of the UEFA Cup.<ref>{{cite news |title=Millwall count Cup cash |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/3600491.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=5 April 2004 |access-date=22 June 2015 }}</ref>

It was the last time that Manchester United won the FA Cup until [[2016 FA Cup Final|2016]].

